body:
In non-strict mode, `update()` returns `null` when a record is missing. The `@objectstack/spec` DriverInterface contract requires `update()` to return a record (non-null), so this behavior is contract-breaking and can cause runtime failures in callers that assume an object. Prefer always throwing on missing records (or adjust the protocol/schema if `null` is meant to be supported).
```suggestion
      this.logger.warn('Record not found for update', { object, id });
      throw new Error(`Record with ID ${id} not found in ${object}`);
```

`MemoryDriverSpec` sets `configSchema: {}` even though `MemoryConfigSchema` defines real configuration options (strictMode, initialData, persistence, indexes, maxRecordsPerObject). With an empty JSON schema, UIs/tools using `DriverDefinitionSchema.configSchema` can’t generate a correct config form. Consider providing a minimal JSON Schema here (or at least documenting that it will be populated from `MemoryConfigSchema` at runtime, consistent with `MongoDriverSpec`).
```suggestion
  configSchema: {
    type: 'object',
    description: 'Memory Driver Connection Configuration',
    additionalProperties: false,
    properties: {
      initialData: {
        type: 'object',
        description: 'Pre-populated data keyed by object name',
        additionalProperties: {
          type: 'array',
          items: {
            type: 'object',
            additionalProperties: true,
          },
        },
      },
      strictMode: {
        type: 'boolean',
        description: 'Throw on missing records instead of returning null',
        default: false,
      },
      persistence: {
        type: 'object',
        description: 'File-system persistence',
        additionalProperties: false,
        properties: {
          filePath: {
            type: 'string',
            description: 'File path to persist data',
          },
          autoSaveInterval: {
            type: 'number',
            description: 'Auto-save interval in ms',
            minimum: 100,
            default: 5000,
          },
        },
        required: ['filePath'],
      },
      indexes: {
        type: 'object',
        description: 'Index configuration per object',
        additionalProperties: {
          type: 'array',
          items: {
            type: 'string',
          },
        },
      },
      maxRecordsPerObject: {
        type: 'integer',
        description: 'Max records per object (memory bound)',
        minimum: 1,
      },
    },
  },
```

`find()` adds field projection by treating `query.fields` as `string[]`, but `QuerySchema` allows `fields` entries to be either strings or objects (relationship field nodes). If a non-string field node is passed, `projectFields()`/`getValueByPath()` will throw at runtime. Consider validating/normalizing `query.fields` to a string-only list for this driver (or explicitly rejecting non-string field nodes with a clear error).
```suggestion
      const nonStringFields = query.fields.filter(field => typeof field !== 'string');
      if (nonStringFields.length > 0) {
        throw new Error(
          'InMemoryDriver.find does not support relationship field nodes in "fields". ' +
            'All entries in "fields" must be strings for this driver.',
        );
      }
      const fieldNames = query.fields as string[];
      results = results.map(record => this.projectFields(record, fieldNames));
```

Despite the PR claiming “immutable returns”, `find()` currently returns references to the in-memory records (except when projection runs). Callers can mutate the returned objects and accidentally modify the underlying store. To make reads immutable, return shallow copies for all records (and ensure `findOne()`/`findStream()` follow the same behavior).
```suggestion
    // 6. Immutability: return shallow copies so callers cannot mutate the store
    const immutableResults = results.map(record => ({ ...(record as any) }));

    this.logger.debug('Find completed', { object, resultCount: immutableResults.length });
    return immutableResults;
```
